= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $x,y$ be the generators associated with the two pairs of <one-handle> feet. Following the attaching circle across the identifications marked $a,b$ and $c,d$ gives the word $xyx^{-1}y^{-1}$, up to reversal and changing generator orientations. Thus the <van Kampen theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(W)=\langle x,y\mid xyx^{-1}y^{-1}=1\rangle\cong\mathbb Z^2.}
$$
The <handle decomposition> has cellular groups $C_0=\mathbb Z$, $C_1=\mathbb Z^2$, $C_2=\mathbb Z$, with no higher groups. The boundary of the two-cell is the abelianized attaching word, which is zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{H_j(W;\mathbb Z)=\begin{cases}\mathbb Z,&j=0,2,\\\mathbb Z^2,&j=1,\\0,&j\ge3.\end{cases}}
$$
The zero framing is the product framing of the <commutator handlebody of the torus>, so the represented manifold is $T^2\times D^2$ and its <intersection form> is $(0)$. In particular its boundary is $T^2\times S^1=T^3$.

The boundary <homology> can also be obtained without using the product identification. By <Poincare-Lefschetz duality>, $H_3(W,\partial W)=H^1(W)=\mathbb Z^2$ and $H_2(W,\partial W)=H^2(W)=\mathbb Z$. The map $H_2(W)\to H_2(W,\partial W)$ is its zero <intersection form>. The <long exact sequence of a pair> then gives split sequences
$$
0\to\mathbb Z^2\to H_2(\partial W)\to\mathbb Z\to0,\qquad
0\to\mathbb Z\to H_1(\partial W)\to\mathbb Z^2\to0.
$$
The boundary is connected and oriented, so its zeroth and third <homology> are both $\mathbb Z$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{H_j(\partial W;\mathbb Z)=\begin{cases}\mathbb Z,&j=0,3,\\\mathbb Z^3,&j=1,2,\\0,&j\ge4.\end{cases}}
$$

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

There are no <one-handles>, so attaching the three <two-handles> to a four-ball cannot create a <fundamental group> generator: **$\pi_1(W)=1$**. The handle <chain complex> has $C_2=\mathbb Z^3$, $C_1=0$ and $C_0=\mathbb Z$, giving
$$
\boxed{H_0(W)=\mathbb Z,\qquad H_2(W)=\mathbb Z^3,\qquad H_j(W)=0\ (j\ne0,2).}
$$
To calculate the boundary, orient the two long components down their central strands, and orient the small component clockwise. The six mutual crossings of the long components have negative sign, giving <linking number> $-3$. Each long component has two positive crossings with the small component, giving <linking number> $1$. The <surgery linking matrix>, in the order of framings $4,3,2$, is consequently
$$
Q=\begin{pmatrix}4&-3&1\\-3&3&1\\1&1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad\det Q=-7.
$$
Changing a component orientation changes the corresponding row and column signs, and does not change the resulting <homology>. The <long exact sequence of a pair> and <Poincare-Lefschetz duality> identify $H_2(\partial W)=\ker Q$ and $H_1(\partial W)=\operatorname{coker}Q$. The determinant is nonzero, so the kernel vanishes. The gcd of matrix entries is one; the two-by-two minors include $-6$ and $7$, so their gcd is also one. The <Smith normal form> therefore has diagonal $1,1,7$, and
$$
\boxed{H_j(\partial W;\mathbb Z)=\begin{cases}\mathbb Z,&j=0,3,\\\mathbb Z/7,&j=1,\\0,&j=2\text{ or }j\ge4.\end{cases}}
$$
In particular the sign of the braid crossings matters: replacing $-3$ by $+3$ while leaving both meridian linkings positive would represent a different link and would give the wrong determinant.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Start with a disk in the <zero-handle> and attach two untwisted bands passing through the two <one-handles>. The four passages around its boundary occur in the order $x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}$, giving an embedded once-punctured <torus> whose boundary is the attaching circle of the <two-handle>. Push its interior into the existing four-dimensional handlebody, keeping its boundary on the attaching locus, and cap it with the <core disk of a handle>. The matching product framing makes the union smooth after rounding its corner. This gives an embedded closed <torus> $T\subset W$.

The <torus> uses the core of the <two-handle> exactly once. Equivalently, it intersects that handle's relative co-core disk transversely in one point. Its cellular two-chain is therefore the generator of $C_2=\mathbb Z$, which is a cycle and is not a boundary because there are no three-cells. Thus
$$
\boxed{[T]\text{ generates }H_2(W)\cong\mathbb Z,\qquad T\cdot T=0.}
$$
This is the zero section in the <commutator handlebody of the torus> $T^2\times D^2$.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Use the boundary-orientation convention in which $-p/q$ surgery on the unknot gives $L(p,q)$. The <negative continued fraction> is
$$
\frac73=3-\frac1{2-1/2}=[3,2,2]^-.
$$
Attach three <two-handles> to a <zero-handle> along a chain of unknots, with adjacent <linking numbers> $+1$ and framings $-3,-2,-2$. Equivalently, take the <plumbing of oriented disk bundles> over three <spheres> with <Euler numbers> $-3,-2,-2$. Successive <slam-dunk moves> change the boundary surgery coefficient to $-3-1/(-2-1/(-2))=-7/3$, proving that its oriented boundary is $L(7,3)$ in this convention.

The zero sections are a basis of second <homology>, and the <intersection form> is
$$
\boxed{Q=\begin{pmatrix}-3&1&0\\1&-2&1\\0&1&-2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Its successive leading principal minors are $-3,5,-7$. Equivalently those of $-Q$ are $3,5,7>0$, so <Sylvester's criterion> proves that $Q$ is negative definite. This explicitly constructs the required filling.

If <lens spaces> are instead labeled by positive surgery with its boundary orientation, use the chain with framings $-2,-4$, whose coefficient is $-7/4$; its boundary is orientation-preservingly the positive-surgery $L(7,3)$, since orientation reversal changes $q=4$ to $q=3$. Its negative-definite form is $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}-2&1\\1&-4\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. This is only a convention change in the named boundary, and specifies a valid filling in either labeling system.

